{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"pandas-analysis","title":"Exploring a Dataset","group":"Pandas","order":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A first pass over any new dataset: look at it, check types and missing values, summarise, filter, group. Here with a small version of the notebook's flower dataset.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye\"></i>First look</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\nprint(df.shape)                                  # (8, 4)\nprint(df.head(2).to_dict(\"records\"))             # [{'species': 'rose', 'size': 'medium', 'fragrance': 'mild', 'height_cm': 48.55}, {'species': 'shoeblack plant', 'size': 'medium', 'fragrance': 'mild', 'height_cm': 147.07}]\nprint(df.dtypes.astype(str).to_dict())           # {'species': 'str', 'size': 'str', 'fragrance': 'str', 'height_cm': 'float64'}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>df.head(n)</code> / <code>df.tail(n)</code></td><td>First / last rows</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.shape</code></td><td>(rows, columns)</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.info()</code></td><td>Column types and non-null counts</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.describe()</code></td><td>Count, mean, std, min, quartiles, max of numeric columns</td></tr><tr><td><code>df[\"col\"].value_counts()</code></td><td>How often each value appears</td></tr><tr><td><code>df.isna().sum()</code></td><td>Missing values per column</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-circle\"></i>Missing values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\nprint(df.isna().sum().to_dict())                 # {'species': 0, 'size': 0, 'fragrance': 1, 'height_cm': 0}\nfilled = df.fillna({\"fragrance\": \"unknown\"})\nprint(filled.loc[6, \"fragrance\"])                # unknown\nprint(len(df.dropna()))                          # 7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Decide per column: <strong>drop</strong> rows (<code>dropna</code>), <strong>fill</strong> with a value or the mean/median (<code>fillna</code>), or keep and flag. Never silently ignore them.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-histogram\"></i>Summaries</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\nprint(df[\"species\"].value_counts().to_dict())     # {'rose': 3, 'shoeblack plant': 3, 'hibiscus': 2}\nprint(round(df[\"height_cm\"].mean(), 2))           # 101.95\nprint(df[\"height_cm\"].describe()[[\"min\", \"max\"]].tolist())   # [35.2, 184.0]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Filtering, sorting and grouping</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\ntall = df[df[\"height_cm\"] &gt; 100]\nprint(tall[\"species\"].tolist())                                   # ['shoeblack plant', 'shoeblack plant', 'hibiscus', 'hibiscus']\nprint(df[(df[\"size\"] == \"medium\") &amp; (df[\"fragrance\"] == \"mild\")].shape[0])   # 3\nprint(df.sort_values(\"height_cm\", ascending=False).iloc[0][\"species\"])       # hibiscus\nprint(df.groupby(\"species\")[\"height_cm\"].mean().round(1).to_dict())\n# {'hibiscus': 155.3, 'rose': 57.3, 'shoeblack plant': 111.0}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Combine conditions with <code>&amp;</code> (and), <code>|</code> (or), <code>~</code> (not) — <strong>not</strong> <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> — and wrap each condition in parentheses.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-column-insert-right\"></i>New columns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nfrom io import StringIO\n\ncsv = \"\"\"species,size,fragrance,height_cm\nrose,medium,mild,48.55\nshoeblack plant,medium,mild,147.07\nshoeblack plant,medium,none,102.93\nhibiscus,large,none,184.00\nshoeblack plant,large,mild,83.07\nrose,small,strong,35.20\nhibiscus,large,,126.69\nrose,medium,mild,88.11\n\"\"\"\ndf = pd.read_csv(StringIO(csv))       # same call you'd use with a file path\n\ndf[\"height_m\"] = (df[\"height_cm\"] / 100).round(2)\ndf[\"is_tall\"] = df[\"height_cm\"] &gt; 100\nprint(df.loc[1, \"height_m\"], df.loc[1, \"is_tall\"])   # 1.47 True</code></pre></figure></section>"}